**Limitation 2: The LLM Cannot Browse, It Can Only Navigate**

This is perhaps the most important limitation to internalise. There is a meaningful difference between browsing a website the way a human would, and navigating to a URL the way DBS GPT does. The LLM powering the web search does not explore a site dynamically. It does not click around, scroll through available options, or figure out what is available before deciding what to retrieve. It goes where it is pointed.

This creates a real problem when the task requires contextual awareness to complete. Take a common scenario: you ask DBS GPT to retrieve the latest report from the MAS website. A human doing this task would land on the page, look through the available dates or publication list, identify the most recent one, and then download it. The LLM cannot do that sequence of steps. It does not know what dates are available because that information only becomes visible through browser interaction. If it cannot infer the exact URL of the latest report from the page content alone, it will struggle, and may either retrieve the wrong file or fail entirely.

This problem gets worse when the page relies on JavaScript-driven interactions to surface data, things like:

- Date pickers or reporting period selectors
- Tab toggles or accordion menus
- Dropdown filters that load different datasets
- Download buttons that only appear after a certain widget state is set

The common thread across all of these is that interacting with them does not change the URL. A human clicking through a date picker and selecting a specific quarter will see the page update, but the web address in the browser bar stays exactly the same. DBS GPT has no way to replicate those interaction steps. It will likely land on the default view of the page, which may not be what you need at all.

